  <content>
  <section><title>Lesson Narrative</title>
<para>In earlier lessons, students wrote and solved linear inequalities in one variable. In this lesson, they transition to linear inequalities in two variables.</para>
<para>Previously, students learned that the solutions to an equation in two variables are all pairs of values that make the equation true, and that, when graphed, the solutions are points on a line. Here, they learn that the solutions to inequalities in two variables also involve pairs of values. When graphed, the solutions are no longer points on a single line, but comprise a region that is bounded by a line. This region consists of all points in a plane on one side of a boundary line. That boundary line is the graph of an equation related to the inequality.</para>
<para>Students begin by noticing that the plots of solutions and non-solutions occupy different parts of a coordinate plane. Next, they think about the boundary line between the two regions and whether it is a part of the solution. Finally, students write some inequalities given graphs that represent solution regions.</para>
<title>Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item>Determine the solution region to an inequality in two variables by testing the points on the line and on either side of the line, given the graph of a related equation.
  </item>
  <item>Explain that the solutions to a linear inequality in two variables are represented graphically as a half-plane bounded by a line.</item>
</list>
<title>Learning Targets (Student Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item>Determine which side of the line the solutions to the inequality will fall, given a two-variable inequality and the graph of the related equation.</item>
  <item>Describe the graph that represents the solutions to a linear inequality in two variables.</item>
</list>
